Available Post-Extraction File Formats

When converting raw OSM data into something actionable, you have a few standard formats to choose from, each suited for different tasks:

    GeoJSON: A geospatial extension of JSON that wraps features in a FeatureCollection. Every object carries its geometry (points, polygons) and properties in a single, standardized structure. It is natively supported by web mapping libraries (like Leaflet or Mapbox) and desktop GIS software (like QGIS), though it can become verbose and memory-heavy for large datasets.

    GeoPackage (.gpkg): A lightweight, SQLite-based database container designed for geographic data. It allows you to run SQL queries, build spatial indexes, and store vector data efficiently in a single file. It is ideal if you plan to query the data relationally later without keeping everything in memory.

    CSV (Comma-Separated Values): A classic tabular format. Great for flat rows of data, though handling nested geographic polygons (like the boundary of a large industrial park) requires encoding geometry as Well-Known Text (WKT) strings.

    JSON Lines (JSONL): A format where every line of the file is a valid, independent JSON object. It combines the structured flexibility of JSON with the stream-processing convenience of flat files.

Evaluating Your Line-Correlated Flat File Approach

Your proposed approach—splitting data attributes into parallel, line-correlated files where line 1 of businesses.txt matches line 1 of gps_coordinates.txt—is a classic flat-file database design pattern. It mirrors how columnar databases and legacy Unix systems optimized storage before modern relational databases or JSON formats took over.

While it demonstrates strong systems-thinking and a desire for clean tabular organization, it carries a major operational risk: index drift and fragility.

If a single script iteration, sorting anomaly, filtering bug, or blank entry causes one file to drop a line or misalign by a single index, the entire relational integrity of your dataset breaks silently. Recovering from that mismatch without unique identifiers (like OSM IDs) becomes extremely difficult.

A safer evolution of your idea is JSON Lines (JSONL) or a single CSV file with proper headers. In JSONL, each line represents a single, self-contained business record containing its name, coordinates, category, and tags all in one object:
JSON

{"id": 123456, "name": "Example Corp", "type": "office", "lat": 42.88, "lon": -78.87}

This preserves the exact line-by-line stream-processing benefits you want (allowing you to read the file line-by-line in code using standard while read loops or Python iterators) while eliminating the risk of file-to-file synchronization errors.
